SYSCALL REFERENCE: FILES, DIRECTORY AND FD

This is a high-level reference for the userland wrappers. For
exact constants/structs use include/piconix_syscall.h and
userland/lib headers in the PicoNIX source tree.

open(path, flags)
  Opens a file or VFS node, returns FD or negative on failure.
  Flags include O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY and O_RDWR; not all nodes are
  writable. /man and /proc are read-only interfaces.

read(fd, buf, count)
  Returns bytes consumed, 0 on EOF, negative on error.
  A device may be endless or blocking. Use bounded count.

write(fd, buf, count)
  Returns bytes written; may write fewer than requested.
  For critical outputs, retry remaining data and close fd.

close(fd)
  Frees FD and device/socket resources; errors must be checked.

lseek(fd, offset, whence)
  Seekable RAM files and selected virtual files support SEEK_SET,
  SEEK_CUR, SEEK_END. Streaming devices may reject seeking.
  /man pages and /proc/sym/kernel support offset access.

fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, flags)
  O_NONBLOCK changes blocking behavior where supported.
  fcntl(fd,F_GETFL,0) queries current flags.

ioctl(fd, cmd, arg)
  Device-specific command; wrong command/argument may fail.
  See /man/i2c-workflow and /man/pwm-workflow.

pipe(fds), dup2(old,new)
  Used by shell pipelines, redirects streams, and MCP command
  output capture. Close unused pipe ends to avoid stalled readers.

getdents(fd,...)
  Enumerates directory entries. ls/tree/find use it; not every
  VFS object is a real disk file. Directory offsets are synthetic.

